BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:india-foundation-arts-research-programme-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: India Foundation for the Arts: Arts Research Programme (M
 ay 2026 Request for Proposals)
DESCRIPTION:IFA's Arts Research Programme funds research into forms of know
 ing and thinking rooted in creative\, scholarly or cultural practice. It e
 xplicitly welcomes experimental and self-reflexive methodologies\, practic
 e-based inquiry\, interdisciplinary dialogue\, community engagement\, tran
 slation studies and work on marginalised narratives\, with a shareable pub
 lic outcome expected. Open to artists\, scholars\, curators\, translators\
 , educators and practitioner-researchers. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: India
 n nationals only\, and PIO and OCI card holders are explicitly excluded\; 
 any collaborators must also be Indian nationals. Applicants need a PAN car
 d and valid ID. PROCESS NOTE: this is a two-stage email application\, not 
 a web form. Applicants were expected to discuss the project concept with t
 he Programme Officer (harshita@indiaifa.org) by 11 July 2026\, with the fi
 nal proposal\, CV and budget breakdown emailed by 10 August 2026 at 23:59 
 IST\, in an email no larger than 25 MB. Proposals may be written in any In
 dian language\, including English.\n\nOrganisation: India Foundation for t
 he Arts (IFA)\nLocation: India\; project-based with no residency requireme
 nt. Open to Indian nationals only.\nAward: Up to INR 500\,000 (around USD 
 5\,700 / EUR 4\,900) per project. Honorarium is capped at 35 percent of th
 e budget. Institutional overhead\, building costs\, infrastructure and equ
 ipment purchases are excluded. No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 12 m
 onths\, maximum 18 months.\n\nMore: https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-
 may2026.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-may2026.html?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:India\; project-based with no residency requirement. Open to India
 n nationals only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultuurloket-digitall-implementatiebijdrage-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultuurloket DigitALL: Implementation Contribution (Imple
 mentatiebijdrage\, 2026)
DESCRIPTION:A one-off 2026 call letting Dutch cultural institutions reuse a
 nd adapt digital technology that other institutions have already built and
  published in the open DigitALL Toolkit\, for example translation software
  shared between theatres or a streaming platform adapted for a new venue. 
 Grants run up to EUR 30\,000 and cover technical modification\, integratio
 n\, production and deployment costs. Eligible applicants are museums\, the
 atres\, music venues\, festivals\, film theatres and professional performi
 ng arts producers. Applications are submitted online and assessed in order
  of receipt rather than comparatively\, with a decision within ten weeks. 
 The deadline was extended from 31 May 2026 to 1 November 2026 after high d
 emand. IMPORTANT RESTRICTION: this route cannot be used to further develop
  your own pilot internally\; it exists specifically to spread another inst
 itution's working technology across the sector. For developing something n
 ew\, use the DigitALL pilot contribution instead.\n\nOrganisation: Cultuur
 loket DigitALL\nLocation: Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingd
 om. No city or region restriction.\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000. Covers techn
 ical modification\, system integration\, production and deployment costs. 
 Hardware purchase alone is ineligible unless clearly essential to the publ
 ic experience. No stated co-financing percentage. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Not specified\; scoped to a single implementation.\n\nMore: https:/
 /cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingdom. No city or reg
 ion restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:innovate-uk-next-wave-createch-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260812
SUMMARY:Deadline: Innovate UK: Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1\, Createch (20
 26)
DESCRIPTION:Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1 is Innovate UK's current open Cre
 atech competition\, investing up to GBP 10 million in early-stage innovati
 on at the intersection of creative technology and the frontier industries 
 named in the Industrial Strategy: video games\, advertising and marketing\
 , film and television\, and music and the performing and visual arts. It f
 unds industrial research to take innovations beyond proof of concept and d
 e-risk future investment\, with individual projects between GBP 100\,000 a
 nd 500\,000 in total costs. Grant rates depend on organisation size\, from
  70 percent for micro and small enterprises down to 50 percent for large o
 rganisations\, so applicants must supply match funding for the remainder. 
 The lead must be a UK-registered SME\; non-profits and CICs are barred fro
 m leading\, though they can participate as collaborators alongside a comme
 rcial lead. Non-funded partners may be based internationally. The competit
 ion opened 23 June 2026 and closes 11 August 2026 at 11:00am UK time. NOTE
  ON FIT: this is an R&D competition for creative-technology product develo
 pment\, not a fund for delivering festivals or exhibitions\, so it suits a
 n organisation whose work can be framed as industrial research with a comm
 ercial SME lead.\n\nOrganisation: Innovate UK\, UK Research and Innovation
 \nLocation: United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, r
 esults exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.\nAward: GBP
  100\,000 to 500\,000 total project costs from a GBP 10 million fund\; no 
 organisation may receive more than GBP 3 million across the programme. Gra
 nt intervention rates for industrial research: up to 70 percent for micro 
 and small enterprises\, 60 percent for medium\, 50 percent for large. Rese
 arch organisations doing non-economic work can claim up to 100 percent\, o
 r 80 percent of full economic cost for academic institutions. The balance 
 is match funding. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 to 12 months\; projects
  must start by 1 January 2027 and end by 31 December 2027.\n\nMore: https:
 //apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/overview/6f
 f2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/ov
 erview/6ff2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, resul
 ts exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:project-liberty-co-lab-fund-round-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260729
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260730
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Liberty: Co-Lab Fund Micro-Grants (Round 2\, 2026
 )
DESCRIPTION:MEMBERS ONLY: this is a member-led collaboration fund\, not an 
 open public call. Projects must be led by a Project Liberty Coalition (All
 iance) member organization. Round 2 shifts to micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, an iterative change from Round 1 intended to spread support acro
 ss more of the Coalition\; organizations that did not receive a Round 1 aw
 ard are preferred. SCOPE: micro-grants fund two kinds of work\, (1) discre
 te projects that strengthen the Coalition itself\, such as Shared Services
  or convenings\, and (2) time-sensitive collaborative projects within a de
 signated workstream. In both cases the work should begin quickly and produ
 ce something concrete. A STRONG APPLICATION SHOWS: work that builds the Co
 alition as an entity\, such as shared tools\, infrastructure or connection
 s that make joint action easier\; clear alignment with a Coalition workstr
 eam\; value for multiple Coalition organizations\; a strong case that the 
 work would not happen\, or would move more slowly\, without the Fund\; a c
 oncrete outcome such as a tool\, resource\, convening\, campaign or resear
 ch product\; and readiness to begin substantive work within two weeks of a
 pproval. LIMIT: one application per organization\, submitted through the e
 xisting Co-Lab Fund application form. Round 1 results were still pending a
 t the time of this call\, with funded projects to be shared soon and engag
 ement opportunities expected across the work. Prospective applicants unsur
 e whether an idea fits this round are invited to reach out first. DEADLINE
 : 29 July 2026 at midnight. Background on the Co-Lab program is in the Coa
 lition's overview document.\n\nOrganisation: Project Liberty (Alliance Ste
 erCo)\; pooled fund supported by Future of Life Institute\, Omidyar Networ
 k\, Ergo Impact\, and Project Liberty\nLocation: Members of the Project Li
 berty coalition (Alliance). Projects led by a Coalition member organizatio
 n\; geography otherwise unrestricted.\nAward: Micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, a deliberate change from Round 1's larger awards so the Fund rea
 ches more of the Coalition. Preference given to organizations that did not
  receive a Round 1 award. No application fee.\nDuration: Short\, discrete 
 projects. Applicants must be ready to begin substantive work within two we
 eks of approval. This is the final round of the summer\; the Fund is expec
 ted to resume later in 2026.\n\nMore: https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTk
 NJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTkNJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Members of the Project Liberty coalition (Alliance). Projects led 
 by a Coalition member organization\; geography otherwise unrestricted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pom-fukuoka-2027-call-for-abstracts@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: POM Fukuoka 2027: Politics of the Machines\, Call for Abs
 tracts
DESCRIPTION:Call for abstracts for the POM (Politics of the Machines) confe
 rence in Fukuoka\, hosted on Kyushu University's Ohashi Campus\, organised
  around the theme of transduction. TRACKS: 01 The Landscape of Transductio
 n: Infrastructural Entanglements of Energy\, Data\, and More-than-Human Li
 fe\; 02 Parasitic Transductions Beyond the Human\; 03 Real Machines: Compu
 tation\, Subjectivity\, and the Politics of Reality-Production\; 04 Techno
 genetic Transductions across Fields of Experience: Affective Politics in/f
 or Times of Crisis\; 05 Transducing Disgust: Bodies\, Technologies\, and t
 he Synthetic Uncanny\; 06 Methoding Transduction: Sound\, Data\, Sonic Res
 earch\; 07 Metabolic Worldviews and Hybrid Intelligences\; 08 Fermentative
  Systems\; 09 Unhearing Bodies: Life as Transduction\; and 10\, an open tr
 ack for Speculations and Culturing Immature Ideas. FORMATS: academic paper
 s\, lecture-performances\, artistic interventions and workshops\, with pro
 posals for alternative formats also encouraged\; indicate the intended for
 mat in the submission. FACILITIES: beyond the Multi Purpose Building and D
 esign Library\, the campus offers a BioFoodLab (a Biolab and a Foodlab\, e
 ach holding roughly 20 people) and an Acoustic Research Center (recording 
 studio for up to 50\, anechoic chamber for up to 15\, echo chamber / rever
 beration room for up to 10\, and semi-anechoic chamber for up to 20)\, whi
 ch the organisers flag as relevant to tracks engaging sound\, food\, perce
 ption\, signal and technological mediation. SUBMIT: an abstract of up to 5
 00 words under one of the tracks\; submissions without a track will be ass
 igned to the most appropriate one. All submissions go through double-blind
  peer review\, and authors of accepted abstracts are invited to present. T
 he submission platform is described as opening soon. AFTER: accepted autho
 rs are invited to submit a full paper of up to 4\,000 words including refe
 rences\, with a formal call for papers to follow. COSTS: note that this is
  a self-funded opportunity. Presenting requires paid conference registrati
 on\, and POM cannot cover travel\, accommodation\, registration or other p
 articipation expenses. DEADLINE: 30 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: POM (Pol
 itics of the Machines) Conference\, hosted at Kyushu University Ohashi Cam
 pus\nLocation: Fukuoka\, Japan (Kyushu University Ohashi Campus\, in perso
 n)\nAward: Unpaid\, and self-funded. POM explicitly cannot provide financi
 al support for travel\, accommodation\, registration or other participatio
 n costs\, and authors of accepted abstracts must register for the conferen
 ce in order to present. No abstract submission fee.\nDuration: Conference 
 in 2027\; accepted authors are later invited to submit a full paper of up 
 to 4\,000 words\n\nMore: https://pom-conference.org/fukuoka-2027/?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pom-conference.org/fukuoka-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fukuoka\, Japan (Kyushu University Ohashi Campus\, in person)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:antikythera-agentworld-journal-cfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Antikythera Journal: Agentworld Special Issue\, Call for 
 Papers
DESCRIPTION:Antikythera\, a research programme on planetary computation and
  the evolution of intelligence\, invites contributions to Agentworld\, a s
 pecial issue of its peer-reviewed MIT Press journal launching Fall 2026. T
 HEME: Agentworld is a research initiative on the medium-term future of hyb
 rid human-AI societies\, framed not around tools for agentic task completi
 on but around what the brief calls 'the human perplexity of deep agentic d
 iffusion'. It treats human societies as the evolutionary niches into which
  new artificial species must adapt\, where the constraints of agentic inte
 gration are qualitative and open-ended (psychological\, philosophical\, li
 nguistic\, symbolic\, existential) rather than utility functions to be sol
 ved. The framing describes a 'parasociety' of densely reticulated social g
 raphs in which agent-to-agent communication constitutes most socially sign
 ificant interaction\, humans and agents anthropomorphise each other by inh
 eritance rather than necessity\, and the two overlapping societies act as 
 mutual evolutionary niches. The disciplinary posture proposed is 'a preemp
 tive anthropology of open-world centaur societies': building the vocabular
 y\, scenarios and conceptual armature for an emergent sociality while it i
 s still forming\, rather than describing it after the fact. WHAT THEY WANT
 : theoretical essays and technical papers that engage the Agentworld theme
 s directly and explain how the contribution adds to the special issue's co
 re questions. Philosophical\, theoretical\, technical\, design-led and pro
 duct-oriented research are all in scope. The call explicitly favours work 
 that exceeds conventional academic journal formats\, including contributio
 ns combining philosophical argument\, technical analysis\, design research
 \, diagrams\, software\, infrastructure proposals\, visual media or web-na
 tive formats. Contributors may optionally propose still images\, diagrams\
 , video\, web-based or interactive components\, and should say whether the
 y have the design\, development or production capacity to realise them\; t
 hese elements are not required. SUBMIT: an abstract or proposal of up to 5
 00 words\; author name(s)\, affiliation(s)\, contact details\, biography(i
 es) and CV(s)\; a designated lead author or contact person for collaborati
 ve submissions\; and a portfolio with at least one relevant work sample su
 ch as a preprint\, published article or paper. Full paper drafts are encou
 raged where available but not required at the initial stage. Submissions m
 ay be individual or collaborative. TIMING: the review window opened 25 Jun
 e 2026. NOTE: no closing date is published on the call page\; the deadline
  shown here is a placeholder set roughly a month out\, so check the site o
 r submit early.\n\nOrganisation: Antikythera (Antikythera: Journal for the
  Philosophy of Planetary Computation\, published with MIT Press)\nLocation
 : Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-forward)
 \nAward: Unpaid. No honorarium or fee is offered for accepted contribution
 s\; the return is peer-reviewed publication in the MIT Press-published Ant
 ikythera Journal. No submission fee.\nDuration: Single publication\; first
  selection of articles published Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://agentworld.ant
 ikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#ca
 ll-for-papers
URL:https://agentworld.antikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral#call-for-papers
LOCATION:Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-fo
 rward)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: EUR 2m funding and learning pro
 gramme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
