UID:dot-line-space-water-bodies-ecology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dot Line Space Art Foundation: Water Bodies & Ecology 202
 6 (Mumbai)
DESCRIPTION:Water Bodies & Ecology 2026 is a 40-day multidisciplinary initi
 ative from the Dot Line Space Art Foundation in Mumbai\, bringing artists\
 , researchers\, collectives and communities together across research activ
 ities\, installations and exhibitions at two venues. The premise is artist
 ic engagement with water\, ecology\, climate\, memory and environmental co
 nsciousness\, and the programme is explicitly after research-based and sit
 e-responsive approaches rather than finished gallery objects\, aiming for 
 dialogue between contemporary practice and ecological concerns. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: individual artists\, artist groups\, interdisciplinary practitioners\
 , community-based initiatives\, and collectives and collaborative practice
 s\, working across all mediums and disciplines. WHAT IS OFFERED: selected 
 artists receive grants towards travel and production expenses. Be aware th
 at the published call does not give amounts\, does not mention an artist f
 ee\, and does not mention accommodation\, so confirm the terms with the fo
 undation (info@dotlinespace.co.in) before committing to travel. Applicatio
 ns go through a Google Form linked from the project page. ABOUT THE HOST: 
 Dot Line Space was co-founded in 2015 by Gourmoni Das\, Piyush Bavalia and
  Shruti Bhosle\, all graduates of Sir J.J. School of Art in Mumbai.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Dot Line Space Art Foundation\nLocation: Mumbai\, India. Presen
 ted across two venues\; the foundation operates out of the New Great Easte
 rn Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fish Art Gallery. Open to artists
  internationally.\nAward: Grants towards travel and production expenses fo
 r selected artists. The call does not publish fixed amounts and does not s
 tate an artist fee or accommodation. No application fee.\nDuration: A 40-d
 ay multidisciplinary programme in 2026 across two Mumbai venues. Deadline 
 30 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-e
 cology-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-ecology-2026/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Mumbai\, India. Presented across two venues\; the foundation opera
 tes out of the New Great Eastern Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fis
 h Art Gallery. Open to artists internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acls-digital-justice-grants-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: ACLS Digital Justice Grants 2026-27 (Seed and Development
 )
DESCRIPTION:ACLS Digital Justice Grants fund digital projects in the humani
 ties and interpretive social sciences that critically engage with the inte
 rests and histories of people of colour and other historically marginalise
 d communities\, including Black\, Latinx\, Indigenous\, disabled and LGBTQ
 + communities. The programme's stated aims are to diversify the digital do
 main\, advance justice and equity in digital scholarly practice\, and cont
 ribute to public understanding of racial and social justice issues\, with 
 an explicit interest in scholars working with under-utilised or understudi
 ed source materials and those at institutions with less infrastructure for
  digital work. TWO TRACKS\, one deadline: SEED GRANTS of USD 10\,000 to 25
 \,000 for projects in the start-up or prototyping phase\, covering plannin
 g\, prototyping and methodology testing\; and DEVELOPMENT GRANTS of USD 50
 \,000 to 100\,000 for projects that have moved past proof of concept and c
 an show preliminary work and evidence of engagement. ELIGIBILITY: at least
  one principal investigator must be a scholar in the humanities or interpr
 etive social sciences\, and awarded funds must be administered by a US ins
 titution of higher education. WHAT IS NOT FUNDED: creative works such as n
 ovels or films\, textbooks\, straightforward translations\, purely pedagog
 ical projects\, and institutional indirect costs. Note that outputs are ex
 pected to be made as widely available as intellectual property allows\, id
 eally under the most liberal open-source or Creative Commons licence. TIMI
 NG: applications go through the ACLS online fellowship and grant administr
 ation system (ofa.acls.org) by 3 November 2026 at 17:00 ET\; grant terms b
 egin between 1 July and 31 December 2027 and run 12 to 18 months. The prog
 ramme is funded by the Mellon Foundation.\n\nOrganisation: American Counci
 l of Learned Societies (ACLS)\, funded by the Mellon Foundation\nLocation:
  Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher education.
  No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principal inves
 tigator.\nAward: Seed Grants USD 10\,000 to 25\,000 for start-up and proto
 typing work\; Development Grants USD 50\,000 to 100\,000 for projects past
  proof of concept. Institutional indirect costs are not covered. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Grant terms run 12 to 18 months\, starting between 1
  July 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline 3 November 2026\, 17:00 ET.\n\nM
 ore: https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher edu
 cation. No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principa
 l investigator.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transfyr-ai-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Transfyr AI Fellowship 2026-2027 (Boston/Cambridge)
DESCRIPTION:A one-year\, full-time research fellowship for graduate student
 s\, recent PhDs and postdocs to lead a project on frontier machine learnin
 g problems grounded in how scientific work actually happens. THE PREMISE: 
 Transfyr argues science is missing an observability layer. Lab work is ful
 l of tacit decisions\, failed attempts\, operator judgment\, noisy instrum
 ents and context that never reaches the written protocol\, and the company
  is building what it calls the world's largest multimodal dataset of scien
 tific execution for models to reason over. Fellows get to work with that d
 ata. RESEARCH AGENDA\, eight named directions: multimodal reasoning (align
 ing video\, audio\, protocol text\, instrument traces\, timestamps\, gaze 
 and outcomes)\; conflicting evidence (uncertainty and calibration\, eviden
 ce attribution\, contradiction-aware reasoning)\; long-context scientific 
 memory (long-horizon state tracking\, retrieval over process histories\, f
 ailure and deviation memory)\; real-world evaluations (benchmark design\, 
 process-level metrics\, lab-grounded model assessment)\; tacit expertise (
 expert-novice comparison\, skill representation\, human-in-the-loop feedba
 ck)\; physical-world AI (embodied data pipelines\, transfer across setting
 s\, policy and assistance evaluation)\; biosecurity and bio-risk observabi
 lity (synthesis-to-execution verification\, emergent capability tracking\,
  autonomous vulnerability detection)\; and scientific execution ontology (
 wet-lab-grounded metadata design\, cross-domain causal attribution\, multi
 -scale variability mapping). WHAT FELLOWS DO: define a research question\,
  build prototypes\, analyse real multimodal scientific-execution data\, pr
 essure-test evaluation ideas\, and produce a publishable or demo-grade art
 ifact\, with direct mentorship from Transfyr's founders\, technical team a
 nd advisor network across AI\, biology\, robotics and scientific operation
 s. WHO CAN APPLY: current graduate students able to take leave (and to sel
 f-certify that they can commit full-time)\, recent PhDs and postdocs in ma
 chine learning\, computer science\, computational biology\, robotics\, hum
 an-computer interaction\, statistics or adjacent fields. Strong ML fundame
 ntals and evidence of research taste are the bar\; the call asks for comfo
 rt with ambiguity and curiosity about how science happens in physical envi
 ronments. International applicants are welcome and visa support is availab
 le for selected fellows. IP TERMS\, worth reading before applying: Transfy
 r owns the fellowship work product and supports publication subject to con
 fidentiality and IP review. This is a company fellowship\, not an academic
  one. APPLICATION: a one-page application sent to ai_fellowship@transfyr.a
 i saying what you want to work on and what resources you need\, with links
  that make your work easy to evaluate\; references are requested of finali
 sts. DEADLINE: applications are reviewed on a ROLLING basis\; apply by 15 
 August 2026 for full consideration\, decisions roll through late August 20
 26\, fellowship begins around September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Transfyr\nL
 ocation: Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remo
 te considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable
 .\nAward: $125\,000 annual compensation plus benefits\, plus access to com
 pute\, GPUs\, data and project support. Applicants are asked to specify th
 e compute\, GPU\, data or support their project would need. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Full-time for 12 months\n\nMore: https://fellowship.trans
 fyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fellowship.transfyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remot
 e considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-special-projects-roles-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: Special Projects Roles in AI Governance (2 to 5 hir
 es)
DESCRIPTION:IAPS is hiring 2 to 5 special projects roles to support or own 
 new workstreams in AI governance\, helping the world navigate towards a po
 sitive future with transformative AI. LEVELS AND SALARY: Special Projects 
 Manager\, USD 122\,000 to 165\,000\; Senior Associate\, USD 97\,000 to 126
 \,500\; Associate\, USD 86\,000 to 101\,000. LOCATION: Washington\, D.C. o
 r the Bay Area\, with London open to exceptional candidates\; IAPS can spo
 nsor US visas. PROJECT AREAS THEY ARE EXCITED ABOUT: (1) Bay Area to DC in
 terface\, closing the gap between where frontier AI is built and where it 
 is governed by surfacing what is happening in AI labs and technical circle
 s for DC policy audiences\, and vice versa\; (2) AI security fieldbuilding
 \, bridging a national security community short on people who deeply under
 stand frontier AI risk and an AI community short on people who understand 
 national security\, through workshops\, convenings and partnerships in DC 
 that put technical experts from both worlds in the same room\; (3) DC-base
 d special projects\, leveraging IAPS's research output and policy networks
  to build talent pipelines and the wider field of AI governance\, drawing 
 on a new 45-seat office in Dupont Circle and a 30-person fellowship cohort
  arriving in DC every six months\; and (4) programs targeting senior polic
 y audiences\, bringing policy entrepreneurs and technical professionals in
 to existing and developing research areas such as institutional resilience
  for advanced AI or AI and defence\, with participants working semi-indepe
 ndently to scope and explore new policy domains. DEADLINE: applications ar
 e considered on a rolling basis\, with applications sent by 8 September 20
 26 prioritised. HOW TO APPLY: apply at iaps.ai/special-projects. Questions
  to careers@iaps.ai.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for AI Policy and Strategy
  (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington\, D.C. or the Bay Area\, USA\; London consid
 ered for exceptional candidates. US visa sponsorship available.\nAward: Sp
 ecial Projects Manager USD 122\,000 to 165\,000\; Senior Associate USD 97\
 ,000 to 126\,500\; Associate USD 86\,000 to 101\,000. Between 2 and 5 role
 s are being filled.\nDuration: Full-time\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/spec
 ial-projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/special-projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C. or the Bay Area\, USA\; London considered for ex
 ceptional candidates. US visa sponsorship available.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:agci-climate-environment-data-stewardship-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260819
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:Deadline: Aspen Global Change Institute: Climate and Environment Da
 ta Stewardship Fellow
DESCRIPTION:AGCI is hiring a Climate and Environment Data Stewardship Fello
 w to coordinate and contribute to cross-organizational climate and environ
 mental data stewardship efforts\, supporting synergies and collective lear
 ning across established initiatives and networks focused on the collection
  and management of data used to inform climate action\, in ways that promo
 te long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperability and security.
  PLACEMENT AND MENTORSHIP: the fellow sits within AGCI's Engaged Research 
 Team\, reporting to AGCI's Research Director\, with day-to-day mentorship 
 from leaders at core partner organisations in the data stewardship space\,
  including the Data Foundation (Sonia Wang) and Public Environmental Data 
 Partners' co-founding member the Open Environmental Data Project (Brittany
  Janis)\, plus guidance from other leaders in the field. RESPONSIBILITIES:
  coordination of the coordinators\, using a network-of-networks approach t
 o build connectedness across emerging and existing work in the climate and
  environmental data landscape and to help the network identify and act on 
 shared priorities\; planning and coordinating network meetings\, workshops
  and other convenings\, starting with consolidating the landscape analysis
  exercises already underway\; elevating data stewardship work by developin
 g and sharing a comprehensive picture of what is needed to sustain data cr
 itical to climate action\; supporting peer learning through surveys\, focu
 s groups or scenario planning that enable cross-organisation conversations
  about possible futures\; connecting evolving data stewardship to the broa
 der climate action and investment ecosystem\; and contributing to the Clim
 ate Reserve\, including professional development programming alongside oth
 er Climate Reserve-supported fellows. REQUIREMENTS: bachelor's degree requ
 ired\, master's or doctorate preferred\; minimum 3 years managing communit
 ies of practice (working groups\, interagency groups and similar) or coord
 inating multi-organisation or network-based initiatives\, ideally related 
 to climate or environmental science or policy\; familiarity with data stew
 ardship principles (long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperabil
 ity\, security) sufficient to support conversations across technical and n
 on-technical partners\; public speaking experience\; experience working ac
 ross institutional\, sectoral and geographic contexts at the science-polic
 y interface\; strong project management\, research\, analysis and synthesi
 s skills\; and willingness to travel domestically and internationally. Dom
 ain expertise in any one data stewardship area is not required\, and knowl
 edge of the civil society data stewardship space is desirable rather than 
 expected. TERM AND PAY: full-time for one year at an anticipated USD 85\,0
 00 to 100\,000. DEADLINE: priority deadline 19 August 2026. HOW TO APPLY: 
 through the AGCI Workable posting.\n\nOrganisation: Aspen Global Change In
 stitute (AGCI)\, with Public Environmental Data Partners\nLocation: Remote
  anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's primary office is i
 n Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' approach. Travel:
  up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international trip.\nAward: Ant
 icipated salary USD 85\,000 to 100\,000\, based on the general US market.\
 nDuration: Full-time for 1 year\; start as soon as possible and no later t
 han 1 October 2026\n\nMore: https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change
 -institute/j/8C263710C9/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change-institute/j/8C263710C9/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's prima
 ry office is in Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' app
 roach. Travel: up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international tri
 p.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:normsy-social-media-measurement-methods-incubator-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Normsy: Social Media Measurement Methods Incubator RFP
DESCRIPTION:Normsy\, a project of Civic Health Project\, runs a small metho
 ds incubator for new strategies to measure or estimate the impact of proso
 cial social-media interventions. Normsy identifies toxic posts (typically 
 content attacking or violating civic norms) and offers real users a set of
  AI-generated prosocial comments to choose from and post\; the open questi
 on is how to estimate the effect of those comments on content producers\, 
 other commenters and bystanders who consume content without interacting me
 asurably. The current approach combines survey experiments (how active X u
 sers respond emotionally to threads with and without the intervention\, an
 d how it shifts agreement with and support for the original post) with pai
 d X API platform data (classifying comments before and after an interventi
 on\, plus coarse engagement metrics that cost money each time they are rec
 ollected). WHAT THEY WANT: a systematic way to leverage available platform
  data and maximise the utility of the survey experiments. Example proposal
 s include a strategy for measuring toxicity/prosociality and reach/visibil
 ity of a thread over time with a randomisation design generating a control
  group of threads\, or an approach to benchmarking the prosocial potential
  of AI-generated comments through an iterative arena with human judges or 
 a theory-backed LLM validation approach with human validation. The brief i
 s explicit that methodological innovation\, causal identification\, scalab
 le measurement frameworks and novel observational approaches matter far mo
 re than proposing new intervention types\; proposals need not study Normsy
  exclusively but must explain how the evaluation design transfers to it. E
 thics and IRB considerations are central and Normsy will collaborate with 
 selected researchers on ethics review and implementation planning. Researc
 hers retain publication independence and IP\; the partnership is expected 
 to produce data for an academic paper. SUBMIT: one zipped folder through t
 he linked Google Form containing a 3-page proposal (design\, work already 
 done\, work still needed to scale and implement)\, a budget document for t
 he USD 20\,000\, a timeline with concrete deliverables inside 6 months\, a
 nd short bios for all team members. Award notifications mid-October 2026. 
 Questions to kylan@civichealthproject.org\; informal and exploratory conve
 rsations welcome.\n\nOrganisation: Normsy (a project of Civic Health Proje
 ct)\nLocation: Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, acade
 mic-adjacent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Res
 earch context is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must inclu
 de X).\nAward: USD 1\,000 awarded to each of the top 5 proposals\, plus up
  to USD 20\,000 to fund R&D and/or testing with one selected partner indiv
 idual or team. Preference for budgets where at least 50 percent goes to re
 search costs (survey respondents\, LLM or X API credits). No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Proposals must set out concrete deliverables within 6 month
 s of starting\; the partnership is framed as the start of a potentially lo
 nger-term collaboration\n\nMore: https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, academic-adj
 acent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Research c
 ontext is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must include X).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:platform-dali-residencies-2027-what-is-real@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Platform Dali Open Call for Residencies 2027: What is rea
 l?
DESCRIPTION:Platform Dali launches its first annual Open Call for Residenci
 es as part of its Research and Creation Programme\, with the inaugural 202
 7 edition built around the question 'What is real?'. Artists of any nation
 ality and any discipline are invited to propose a research and creation re
 sidency project developed through close engagement with one of Platform Da
 li's partner scientific institutions: the Barcelona Supercomputing Center 
 (BSC-CNS)\, ICFO (the Institute of Photonic Sciences)\, the Institute of M
 arine Sciences (ICM-CSIC)\, the Institute for High Energy Physics (IFAE)\,
  and the ecosystem of the Barcelona Biomedical Research Park (PRBB)\, incl
 uding the Centre for Genomic Regulation (CRG) and EMBL Barcelona. Five art
 ists are selected\, each hosted by one of the partner institutions. WHAT T
 HE PROGRAMME EXPECTS: research is treated as a creative practice in its ow
 n right\, and residents are explicitly not expected to arrive with a fully
  defined artwork. The residency is designed to support an initial phase of
  open-ended enquiry inside a scientific research environment\, letting que
 stions\, methods and ideas emerge through dialogue and experimentation\; t
 hat research phase then informs the development and production of a new ar
 twork\, so research and creation run as sequential\, interconnected stages
 . TIMELINE: open call launched 2 July 2026\; applications close 7 Septembe
 r 2026 at 23:59 CET\; selected artists notified by November 2026\; residen
 ts publicly announced 2 December 2026. Two residency periods are available
 \, February to April 2027 and September to November 2027\, with final date
 s agreed between Platform Dali\, the host scientific institution and each 
 resident. Full application criteria\, residency structure and terms and co
 nditions are on the open call page.\n\nOrganisation: Platform Dali\nLocati
 on: Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scientifi
 c institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.\nAward: Artist fee of 
 EUR 7\,500 per selected artist\, plus access to a production budget of up 
 to EUR 25\,000 for the development of a new artwork. Five artists are sele
 cted. For artists based outside Barcelona\, Platform Dali covers travel\, 
 accommodation\, subsistence and travel insurance for both the introductory
  visit and the on-site residency\, within a predefined budget. Also provid
 ed: curatorial support from Platform Dali and the partner institution's te
 am throughout the residency\, and access to the host institution's facilit
 ies\, expertise and research communities\, subject to that institution's r
 egulations and operational requirements. For collectives\, the artist fee\
 , production budget and covered expenses are shared among members\, and a 
 maximum of two members may take part in the introductory visit and on-site
  residency.\nDuration: One of two residency periods: February to April 202
 7\, or September to November 2027. Final dates are agreed between Platform
  Dali\, the host institution and the resident.\n\nMore: https://platformda
 li.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is-real/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://platformdali.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is
 -real/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scien
 tific institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bold-lab-bold-fellow-oxford-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: BOLD Fellow\, British Open-ended Learning and Discovery L
 ab
DESCRIPTION:The British Open-ended Learning and Discovery (BOLD) Lab is a n
 ew UKRI-funded initiative establishing an AI research laboratory led from 
 the University of Oxford\, bringing more than 80 researchers from Oxford\,
  UCL and Imperial College London under one vision. It carries an initial i
 nvestment of around GBP 8 million with the potential for a further GBP 22 
 million extending the investment over six years\, making it one of the UK'
 s largest bets on fundamental AI research. The lab's remit is ambitious\, 
 high-risk work on the fundamental science of artificial intelligence\, alo
 ngside training future leaders in the field. THE ROLE: up to 12 BOLD Fello
 ws are being recruited across the three universities. Fellows are expected
  to operate with a high level of independence and responsibility\, setting
  their own research direction\, publishing at the highest level\, and ment
 oring junior researchers and doctoral students. They also play a leading r
 ole in managing the lab and take responsibility for developing its strateg
 ic and scientific direction\, with primary responsibility for a programme 
 of independent funded research projects. The posting frames the draw as ex
 ceptional independence\, direct collaboration with world-leading colleague
 s across the three institutions\, access to substantial and rapidly growin
 g compute\, and real influence over the intellectual direction and culture
  of a major national lab. WHO SHOULD APPLY: senior researchers with an est
 ablished\, original research track record in AI or a closely related field
 \, who are a recognised authority in their area. HOW TO APPLY: submit an o
 nline application with a covering letter or supporting statement\, a CV\, 
 and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed beforehand to
  the BOLD Exec Team at boldexec-team@eng.ox.ac.uk. NOTE: this is a salarie
 d fixed-term research post rather than a grant. Only applications received
  before midday on Tuesday 15 September 2026 can be considered.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: University of Oxford\, Department of Engineering Science (BOLD Lab\,
  with UCL and Imperial College London)\nLocation: Oxford\, UK\, with posts
  also based at UCL and Imperial College London. The 12 posts are employed 
 across the three universities.\nAward: Salary on Oxford Grade 10R\, GBP 65
 \,336 to GBP 84\,663 per year\, with the posting noting that further salar
 y options are being explored. Benefits include associate membership at Reu
 ben College\, 38 days annual leave\, pension scheme\, childcare services\,
  and travel expenses for stakeholder and conference visits. Fellows also g
 et access to substantial compute: 3 million GPU hours on Isambard for the 
 first 18 months\, with the lab working towards 5\,000 H100-equivalents in 
 total across industry and government support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Fixed-term\, 12 months\, funded by UKRI\n\nMore: https://eng.ox.ac.uk/j
 obs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Oxford\, UK\, with posts also based at UCL and Imperial College Lo
 ndon. The 12 posts are employed across the three universities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:next-art-ai-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI ART AWARD: NEXT ART AI 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEXT ART AI is an open AI art competition run by the HOBAN Cult
 ural Foundation\, welcoming participants of all backgrounds regardless of 
 career\, nationality or age\, and framed as a way to reflect on the future
  of AI art through new modes of expression and artistic experimentation. T
 WO CATEGORIES: AI IMAGE ART\, for works in a single-image format at 150dpi
  or higher\, submitted as JPG\, JPEG\, PNG or BMP up to 10MB\; and AI VIDE
 O ART\, open to a wide range of video formats including animation\, motion
  graphics and live-action footage enhanced or transformed using AI\, at 10
 80p or higher\, under one minute\, 30 or 60fps recommended\, stereo 48kHz 
 audio recommended\, as mp4 (h.264) or mov. Video entries are submitted as 
 a URL via YouTube\, Vimeo\, Dropbox or OneDrive\, or an alternative platfo
 rm if those are restricted in your region\; links that are inaccessible du
 e to privacy settings may be excluded from judging. WHO CAN ENTER: individ
 uals or teams of up to three including the representative\, though you may
  not submit both as an individual and as part of a team. Team representati
 ves create the account in their own name and all members submit a consent 
 form for personal data. Applicants under 19 need a guardian's signed conse
 nt. One work may be submitted per category. RULES ON AI AND ASSETS: work m
 ust be original and created by the applicant or team\, and must be image o
 r video art created using generative AI platforms. Works that have previou
 sly won an award in another competition are not eligible. Free or paid ext
 ernal assets\, game engines\, AI frameworks\, open-source tools and editin
 g software may be used freely\, but applicants must hold licences permitti
 ng commercial use for every element\, and work infringing third-party righ
 ts is excluded. Even where external assets and editing software are used\,
  the creative process must involve AI\, whether AI-assisted direction\, tr
 ansformation\, generation or other AI-based processes\, and the choice of 
 AI tools\, the scope and method of their use\, and the prompts themselves 
 are all weighed in evaluating the creative use of AI. TIMELINE: applicatio
 ns open 1 September 2026 and close 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm KST\, prel
 iminary round early October\, final round late October\, announcement and 
 ceremony early November\, exhibition late November 2026. Organised and hos
 ted by the HOBAN Cultural Foundation\, sponsored by HOBAN Group\, with Seo
 ul Shinmun and EBN as media partners. Details and schedule are subject to 
 change at the organiser's discretion.\n\nOrganisation: HOBAN Cultural Foun
 dation\nLocation: Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibit
 ion in South Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
 \nAward: Total prize pool of KRW 21\,000\,000 across 20 winners\, paid aft
 er deduction of applicable taxes and public charges. Grand Prize KRW 5\,00
 0\,000 (selected regardless of category)\; in each of the two categories\,
  First Prize KRW 2\,000\,000\, three Second Prizes of KRW 1\,000\,000 each
 \, and five Honorable Finalist awards of KRW 500\,000 each\; plus a HOBAN 
 Group President's Award of KRW 1\,000\,000. Winners also receive plaques a
 nd certificates\, online and offline exhibition opportunities\, and a feat
 ure on the official online platform. No entry fee is mentioned.\nDuration:
  One-off competition\; exhibition late November 2026\n\nMore: https://next
 -art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://next-art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibition in Sou
 th Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:epic-megagrants-cycle-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Epic MegaGrants 2026 (Cycle 2)
DESCRIPTION:Epic MegaGrants are financial grants from Epic Games for creati
 ve\, noteworthy and innovative projects built in and around Unreal Engine\
 , or projects that enhance the open source 3D graphics ecosystem. Over 2\,
 000 grants have been awarded to date. The programme is explicitly aimed at
  smaller teams\, solo developers and individuals as well as studios: Epic 
 states that individuals and small teams are eligible and encouraged\, and 
 that it treats every project equally regardless of who you are. WHAT IS FU
 NDED: (1) INDIE GAMES\, small or solo teams working in Unreal Engine\, sub
 mitted past the idea and design phase with at least some working prototype
 \, ideally with a video link\; (2) UEFN EXPERIENCES\, Fortnite Creative is
 lands and interactive experiences built in Unreal Editor for Fortnite\; (3
 ) ENGINE MIGRATION\, a proportion of MegaGrants is reserved for teams movi
 ng a partially developed project from another engine to Unreal\; (4) FILM\
 , TV AND LIVE EVENTS\, including live-action and animated films and shows\
 , broadcast\, live and location-based events\; (5) OTHER NON-GAMES VERTICA
 LS such as architecture\, automotive\, manufacturing\, simulation\, produc
 t design\, advertising and VR/AR\, where more detailed project plans and p
 rototypes are expected\; (6) EDUCATION\, including research\, curricula an
 d course development and university programmes\, though for institutions E
 pic accepts submissions only from degree-granting schools and educators at
  such schools\; (7) TOOLS AND OPEN SOURCE\, creating open source tools or 
 enhancing open source software using Unreal Engine. NON-UNREAL PROJECTS AR
 E ELIGIBLE if you want to move them to Unreal Engine\, or if the project e
 nhances open source 3D content creation whether or not it relates to Unrea
 l Engine. EVALUATION rests on three things: quality and unique appeal (hig
 h-quality demos or builds\, well-documented descriptions\, technical ambit
 ion)\; community benefit (how you will give back\, for example open-sourci
 ng the project\, releasing assets through a marketplace\, sharing learning
 s\, or shipping an SDK\, tool or plug-in)\; and viability (a clear budget 
 with major spend areas and a project plan with milestones\, deliverables a
 nd timeline). NOTE ON DEADLINES: the FAQ still describes MegaGrants as an 
 ongoing programme with no specific deadline\, but the timeline published o
 n the same page now runs in fixed cycles\, and the Cycle 2 submission wind
 ow closes 4 September 2026 at 23:59 ET. Treat the cycle date as the operat
 ive deadline. You may apply for more than one project\, and may reapply fo
 r the same project after a decision if you have made updates. Epic is not 
 offering sponsorships and cannot give feedback on applications. Apply at m
 egagrants.unrealengine.com.\n\nOrganisation: Epic Games\nLocation: Open wo
 rldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to you\, you are 
 eligible.\nAward: Typical grants range from USD 5\,000 to USD 75\,000\, wi
 th some exceptional projects receiving up to USD 150\,000. Epic states tha
 t recipients continue to own their IP and may publish it however they wish
 \, so this is a grant rather than an investment or equity deal. Grants up 
 to and including USD 25\,000 may be paid in a single instalment\; larger g
 rants are paid across several instalments tied to continued progress. Fund
 s are for project and team expenses such as development and hiring\; hardw
 are purchases or rentals are approved only occasionally\, case by case. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Cycle 2 of 2026 runs 29 June to 11 December 2
 026. Submission window 29 June to 4 September 2026 (closes 23:59 ET)\, rev
 iew 5 September to 4 December 2026\, notices 7 to 11 December 2026. Cycle 
 1 ran 12 January to 19 June 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.unrealengine.com/me
 gagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.unrealengine.com/megagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open worldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to
  you\, you are eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:les-antipodes-numeric-2026-the-word@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Numeric 2026: The Word (Les antipodes Research and Creati
 on Residency)
DESCRIPTION:Two research and creation residency grants in Agullana\, Catalo
 nia\, for artists\, thinkers and scientists of any branch (formal\, natura
 l\, social\, applied) working at the intersection of art\, culture\, scien
 ce and thought. THIS YEAR'S THEME\, The Word\, is squarely about large lan
 guage models. The framework moves from the word as creative principle acro
 ss traditions (the Judeo-Christian creating God\, the Quran as uncreated w
 ord\, the Sanskrit Om and the Vedas\, mantras that summon rather than desc
 ribe) to science relocating that intuition in the cell (Sagan's four-lette
 r alphabet of DNA as a material text\, Margulis countering that the langua
 ge of life is chemistry\, Witzany holding that all cell interactions are s
 ign-mediated)\, and then to LLMs\, which for the first time detach the wor
 d from the flesh and generate text through a probabilistic architecture tr
 ained on human language. The call asks what LLMs reveal about authorship\,
  originality and meaning when a system generates text that moves\, persuad
 es or deceives without experience or intention\, and whether we can still 
 say the models operate exclusively on human experience once they ingest th
 eir own output. It explicitly wants work that blurs comprehension and simu
 lation\, authorship and recombination\, and it names antecedents worth kno
 wing before applying: Ramon Llull's Ars Magna\, Joan Brossa's Sextina cibe
 rnetica\, Oulipo constraint writing\, oracles and divination\, oral-formul
 aic transmission\, translation as probabilistic approximation\, exquisite 
 corpses\, Burroughs's cut-ups\, automatic writing\, musical improvisation 
 and scientific simulation. Les antipodes asks for engagement 'beyond the i
 nstrumental use of the tools' and away from utopian and dystopian framings
 . WHAT YOU GET: spacious\, luminous\, independent work spaces plus a share
 d terrace in the centre of the village\, spaces for meeting\, dialogue and
  public presentation\, introductions to local\, academic\, artistic and pr
 ofessional contacts on request\, and support organising any activities tha
 t arise. The residency ends with an informal presentation\, and results ma
 y feed into the publications or public programme of Les antipodes. IMPORTA
 NTLY\, this is not an acquisition prize and involves no transfer of rights
 : authorship and intellectual property stay with the creators\, and select
 ion implies no acquisition of the work. Credits on resulting work must ack
 nowledge Numeric 2026 and its collaborators. WHO CAN APPLY: open to everyo
 ne of legal age\, any discipline or origin\, individually or as a collecti
 ve\, with the stipend shared among collective members. Applications must b
 e in Catalan or English whatever the working language of the project\, sub
 mitted through the online form with an optional PDF of up to 5 pages and 1
 0 MB. One application per person or collective. EVALUATION\, in order of w
 eight: singularity and innovation\; relevance to this year's LLM framework
 \; and interaction and transfer of value\, meaning relations with the soci
 al and geographical environment of Agullana and the Emporda and a plan for
  presenting the outcome in editorial\, on-site\, performative\, sound\, au
 diovisual or other form. The call may be declared void if no proposal reac
 hes the required level. PRACTICAL: residents must hold their own health co
 ver (EHIC for EU citizens\, or valid travel or health insurance for Spain 
 otherwise) and are responsible for their own equipment. Supported by the G
 eneralitat de Catalunya\, Agullana and Figueres town councils\, and Accion
  Cultural Espanola through the PICE national residencies programme. Terms 
 are published in Arabic\, Catalan\, English\, Spanish\, French and Chinese
 .\n\nOrganisation: Les antipodes\, with Bolit Centre d'Art Contemporani Gi
 rona and Museu de l'Emporda Figueres\nLocation: Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, C
 atalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 people\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vi
 lafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km from Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\
 , 160 km from Barcelona.\nAward: Two grants. Each provides a stipend of EU
 R 1\,000 as taxable base (taxes and withholdings apply)\, round-trip trave
 l within the European Union\, and accommodation and meals for the four wee
 ks in Agullana. Travel and meals cover the resident only\, though the shar
 ed apartment can host immediate family by arrangement. Numeric also provid
 es the resources specified in the application and agreed during preparatio
 n. No application fee. Note that if a selected candidate lacks the profess
 ional registration needed to receive the stipend and payment has to be arr
 anged through Les antipodes\, the resulting costs are deducted from the st
 ipend.\nDuration: Four weeks\, 2 to 30 November 2026\, with both residenci
 es running simultaneously. Preceded by one month of preparation and follow
 ed by a two-month closing period. Applications 23 July to 8 September 2026
 \; decision announced 22 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://lesantipodes.com
 /numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://lesantipodes.com/numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, Catalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 peopl
 e\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vilafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km f
 rom Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\, 160 km from Barcelona.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kultur-ensemble-palermo-residencies-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kultur Ensemble Palermo Residencies 2027 (Franco-German D
 uos)
DESCRIPTION:Kultur Ensemble Palermo\, the joint Franco-German cultural inst
 itute uniting the Institut francais Palermo and the Goethe-Institut Palerm
 o since 2021\, offers two three-month residencies promoting research and c
 reation on Franco-German projects with a close link to Palermo and Sicily.
  Residents work from the Bottega\, the 115 sq m artist's studio inaugurate
 d in October 2024 within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa. WHO CAN APPLY\,
  and this is the unusual constraint: applications must come from a FRANCO-
 GERMAN DUO\, meaning French or German nationality or working in France or 
 Germany\, and all applicants must be UNDER 36 at the time of the residency
 \, a requirement of the Franco-German Youth Office which funds the scheme.
  Beyond that\, applicants need a practice or project that genuinely justif
 ies the need for a studio\, and a project related to Palermo\, Sicily or t
 he Mediterranean region. COMMITMENTS: participate in at least two educatio
 nal activities such as a presentation of work or a workshop with Palermo s
 chools\; provide communication material for the institutes' social media a
 nd take part in a short video interview\; be free from other professional 
 commitments during the residency\; and hold civil liability insurance and 
 health insurance valid abroad for the full period. A public presentation e
 vent is NOT mandatory and can be discussed with the institutes during the 
 stay. The institutes also help establish contacts with the local cultural 
 network and institutions. APPLYING: all documents in a single PDF\, submit
 ted in duplicate\, one version in French or German and one in English\, to
  kulturensemblepalermo@gmail.com. Required: CV of maximum 2 pages per pers
 on for everyone involved including contact details and date of birth\; a p
 roject description with justification for the studio\, maximum 1 page\; an
 d a portfolio of maximum 10 pages total. An Italian-French-German jury of 
 experts decides at the end of September 2026\, with results announced by e
 mail and published on Kultur Ensemble Palermo's Instagram and the social c
 hannels of both institutes. NOTE ON DATES: the official English call PDF s
 ays 'two three-month residencies in 2026'\, which is an error carried into
  several republished versions. The residency periods listed in the same do
 cument\, and the French institute's own page\, both give 2027. Enquiries: 
 bianca.bozzeda@goethe.de (Goethe-Institut) or judith.testault@institutfran
 cais.it (Institut francais).\n\nOrganisation: Kultur Ensemble Palermo (Ins
 titut francais Palermo and Goethe-Institut Palermo)\, with the Franco-Germ
 an Youth Office (OFAJ/DFJW)\nLocation: Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bot
 tega\, a 115 sq m space within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared w
 ith other residents.\nAward: Two residencies. Each provides a residency gr
 ant of EUR 1\,500 per person per month\, one return journey between home a
 nd Palermo capped at EUR 500 per person\, a budget of up to EUR 500 for re
 search\, creation and production costs (agreed in advance with the institu
 tes)\, and accommodation in the city with private rooms and shared common 
 spaces. Over three months that is EUR 4\,500 per person in grant alone\, a
 nd duos are funded per person. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months
 . Two slots in 2027: 11 January to 4 April\, and 20 September to 12 Decemb
 er. Deadline 6 September 2026 at midnight\; jury decision end of September
  2026.\n\nMore: https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze
 -il-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze-il-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bottega\, a 115 sq m space within 
 the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared with other residents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-academic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Academic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The academic track of the ECF Fellowship Programme\, which fost
 ers new research engaging with the history of the European Cultural Founda
 tion and the broader role of culture in European integration. The ECF was 
 established in 1954 by figures including Denis de Rougemont\, Robert Schum
 an\, Hendrik Brugmans and Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands\, on the beli
 ef that Europe will not be built by treaties alone. Over seventy years it 
 has produced programmes\, institutes\, campaigns and collaborations\, incl
 uding a contribution to shaping and implementing the ERASMUS student mobil
 ity scheme. That legacy is now preserved at the Historical Archives of the
  European Union in Florence alongside the records of the EU's main institu
 tions\, a placement that itself recognises the Foundation's role in Europe
 an integration. ELIGIBILITY for the Academic Research Fellowship: research
 ers in European studies\, cultural studies\, history and related fields ac
 ross the humanities and social sciences\, specifically PhD and postdoctora
 l researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of completing a 
 master's or doctoral degree\, and independent scholars with an active rese
 arch profile. Fellows work at the HAEU embedded in the international schol
 arly community of the European University Institute\, with the archives ho
 used at Villa Salviati. Applications and all supporting documentation must
  be submitted in English through the online form. NOTE: this is the EUR 5\
 ,000 academic track. The EUR 10\,000 Artistic Research Fellowship\, open t
 o professional creatives in visual arts\, performing arts\, film and audio
 visual practices\, podcasting and audio storytelling\, documentary\, journ
 alism and literary practices\, is listed separately on the desk and shares
  the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation\, hosted
  at the Historical Archives of the European Union\nLocation: Historical Ar
 chives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Florence\, Italy\, within 
 the European University Institute.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 grant to support a r
 esearch stay at the HAEU\, covering travel\, accommodation and living expe
 nses. The parallel Artistic Research Fellowship carries EUR 10\,000 and ad
 ditionally covers artistic production. No application fee.\nDuration: Rese
 arch stay at the HAEU. Outcomes must be completed and publicly presented o
 r published within two years of signing the Grant Agreement. Deadline 30 S
 eptember 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-ca
 lls/new-fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Historical Archives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Flore
 nce\, Italy\, within the European University Institute.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-ecosystems-living-communities-vol3@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ecosystems as Living Communities vol. III: UniCredit Grou
 p UNIDEE Connective Residencies 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Third edition of Ecosystems as Living Communities\, supporting 
 two production residencies for emerging international artists working with
  socially engaged practices that address contemporary ecological issues. T
 WO PLACES\, and the split matters: one Italian artist and one internationa
 l artist residing in Europe. The framing follows the mission of Fondazione
  Pistoletto Cittadellarte\, the vision and work of Michelangelo Pistoletto
 \, and the UN Sustainable Development Goals\, and promotes interdisciplina
 ry collaboration across scientific\, technological and humanistic fields. 
 WHAT THEY WANT: projects that investigate ecosystems as interconnected com
 munities of humans and non-humans\, challenging anthropocentric perspectiv
 es and promoting an ecocentric mindset\, and that foster positive and rege
 nerative visions of desirable futures rather than only diagnosing crisis. 
 HOW IT RUNS: the two artists share a research phase including meetings wit
 h tutors\, researchers and experts as well as field visits\, so a collabor
 ative attitude and flexibility as the research evolves are described as es
 sential. Resulting works are publicly presented at Cittadellarte in Biella
  and disseminated online. IMPORTANT ON RIGHTS: the final artwork produced 
 during the residency BECOMES PART OF THE UNICREDIT ART COLLECTION\, so thi
 s is a production residency that ends in acquisition by a corporate collec
 tion\, not a no-strings creation grant. Weigh that against the fee before 
 applying. For large installations\, artists are also required to develop a
  simplified version of the work that can be installed in smaller exhibitio
 n spaces while preserving its conceptual integrity. APPLYING: complete the
  online form and submit the required documents\, all in English\, includin
 g a portfolio and CV as one PDF up to 5 MB containing a selected portfolio
  of maximum 5 projects or artworks most fitting to the call topic\, plus a
  brief biography and CV.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadella
 rte and UNIDEE Residency Programs\, in collaboration with UniCredit Group\
 nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.\nAward: Two residencies. Each p
 rovides a gross artist fee of EUR 6\,500\, a production budget of up to EU
 R 2\,500 for costs directly related to producing the final artwork (instal
 lation supports\, equipment rental\, materials)\, round-trip travel and re
 search trips with a combined budget of EUR 1\,000 to 1\,500 per artist dep
 ending on distance\, accommodation in a single room with shared facilities
  at Cittadellarte plus a per diem of EUR 25 per day for up to 60 days\, an
 d a research budget of up to EUR 500 for fees to experts contributing to t
 he research. Cittadellarte separately covers technical support for the exh
 ibition installation\, project communication\, and a publication accompany
 ing the residency and final exhibition. No application fee.\nDuration: Res
 idencies take place between December 2026 and April 2027. The structure an
 d schedule are tailored to each project and defined collaboratively with t
 he artists. Deadline 6 September 2026 at 23:59\; shortlisted candidates ma
 y be interviewed online between late September and mid-October\; selection
  by mid-October 2026.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.ht
 ml?id=263&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=263&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-visiting-research-curator-2027-28@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: UNIDEE Residency Programs: Visiting Research Curator 2027
 -28
DESCRIPTION:Cittadellarte is recruiting a Visiting Research Curator to shap
 e the research arc of the UNIDEE Residency Modules for 2027-2028\, the rol
 e most recently held by Nina Fiocco and Gaia Martino. Founded by Michelang
 elo Pistoletto in 1999\, the residency has hosted thousands of artists and
  cultural professionals from 52 countries. WHAT THEY WANT: rather than a f
 ixed theme set in advance\, they want a curator who brings their own line 
 of inquiry and develops it collectively through the modules. Suggested the
 matic areas are the evolving landscape of socially engaged art practices\,
  research-driven and process-oriented practice\, and the conditions of cul
 tural work and cultural workers\, aligned with UNIDEE's focus on artistic 
 research in response to ecological\, geopolitical and technological challe
 nges. They also flag a specific interest in how contemporary art practices
  are archived\, drawing on the UNIDEE archive\, which documents over two d
 ecades of socially engaged art initiatives. THIS IS A JOB\, NOT A GRANT: t
 he duties list is long and operational. It covers proposing the overarchin
 g curatorial theme\, designing and delivering a minimum of 8 weekly reside
 ntial modules per year\, writing the curatorial statement and resident ope
 n call\, recruiting mentors and guests\, selecting residents\, hosting and
  facilitating every module in person\, communications and website texts\, 
 a dissemination strategy\, and conceiving and editing publications. There 
 is also teaching-adjacent work with the Unidee Academy of Contemporary Art
 s\, including public lectures and studio visits. Budget the time before ap
 plying. PROFILE: experience curating art in the public sphere and socially
  engaged art\, specialised knowledge of international contemporary art\, e
 xperience designing experimental and collaborative educational activities\
 , and experience with artistic research and dissemination standards. Excel
 lent English required\; Italian welcome but not essential. APPLYING: onlin
 e form with a project proposal of maximum 5000 characters stating your vis
 ion for the modules programme and a development strategy including possibl
 e mentors and guests\, plus a CV\, a portfolio of maximum three relevant p
 rojects\, and TWO LETTERS OF RECOMMENDATION\, so line up your referees ear
 ly. A jury of the UNIDEE Advisory Board\, the Director of Cittadellarte an
 d invited experts decides. They do not give feedback on unsuccessful propo
 sals.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadellarte and UNIDEE Resi
 dency Programs\nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-pers
 on presence at Cittadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full leng
 th of each module.\nAward: Compensation and contractual terms commensurate
  with the candidate's level of professional experience\, starting from a g
 ross amount of EUR 22\,000 for the appointment\, excluding travel and hosp
 itality\, which are covered separately. No application fee.\nDuration: Tem
 porary appointment from November 2026 to December 2028\, covering the 2027
 -2028 research arc and a minimum of 8 residency modules per year. Deadline
  13 September 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; shortlisted candidates interviewed onli
 ne by mid-October 2026\, with the selected candidate announced a few days 
 later.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-person presence at Cit
 tadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full length of each module.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-earth-immersion-trembling-planet-2027@artificialno
 uveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening x KNMI: Earth Immersion\, Trembling 
 Planet Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an artist\, designer or maker working with so
 und\, exploring spatial sound in relation to seismology\, climate and disc
 harge-related acoustics\, run with the Royal Netherlands Meteorological In
 stitute. THE PREMISE: climate change is making major landslides\, ice-brea
 koff events from marine-terminating glaciers\, and sound-producing ice mov
 ements more common. These generate infrasonic vibrations inaudible to huma
 ns that sometimes spread across the entire planet and into the atmosphere.
  The residency asks how immersive sound techniques can make these events t
 angible and build meaningful sonic narratives from them. WHAT YOU GET ACCE
 SS TO: scientific data on seismological events\, vibrations\, infrasound a
 nd the movement of land and ice masses\, with scientific expertise from KN
 MI (lead researcher Jelle Assink) and artistic guidance from the Futures o
 f Listening team\, relating local events to global impact. RESEARCH QUESTI
 ONS to position your proposal against include how immersive sound can cult
 ivate situated listening to ecological processes and more-than-human encou
 nters\, how artistic and scientific methods of working with environmental 
 data can inform each other\, and what is gained\, lost or ethically at sta
 ke when environmental sounds are translated\, interpreted or reconstructed
  through spatial and synthetic means. DELIVERABLE: residents are expected 
 to create a final spatial sound production using the 4DSOUND system. Other
  outcomes may include immersive installations\, data-driven sonic works\, 
 audiovisual pieces\, software prototypes or reusable methodologies. Experi
 ence in art-science environments is welcome but not required\; you may ins
 tead explain why working in one would benefit your practice. HARD ELIGIBIL
 ITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherlands AND registered as an
  artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). This is not negotiable a
 nd rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listening is a Netherlands-base
 d platform for spatial sound\, immersive experience\, performance and inst
 allation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek and Art of
  Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supported by CIIIC and Stimu
 leringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listening (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Rob
 in Koek\, Art of Sound) in collaboration with KNMI\, the Royal Netherlands
  Meteorological Institute\nLocation: Partially self-hosted\, partially at 
 the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterdam\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Ut
 recht\, Netherlands.\nAward: Total residency fee of EUR 7\,125\, calculate
 d as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285. Six of those 25 days c
 an be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with their team. Some additional
  financial support is available if needed\, including material and travel 
 costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agreed before the residency 
 starts. No application fee.\nDuration: January to March 2027\, structured 
 as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://fibe
 r.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Partially self-hosted\, partially at the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterd
 am\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Utrecht\, Netherlands.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-architecture-on-sound-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Architecture on Sound Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an individual practitioner\, a duo or a studi
 o working at the intersection of sound and space\, exploring architecture 
 through spatial audio. THE PREMISE: architecture is traditionally conceive
 d through form\, structure\, material and light\, with sound treated as so
 mething to be controlled or optimised afterwards. This residency asks what
  happens if architecture begins with sound\, shifting the question from ho
 w a space should sound to how sound can generate space. Spatial audio\, vi
 rtual acoustics and immersive media now allow prototyping of responsive en
 vironments\, impossible architectures and sonic spaces beyond physical con
 straints. RESEARCH QUESTIONS include how sound can become a first principl
 e in architectural and spatial design\, how the acoustic properties of spa
 ce inform the creation and perception of spatial sound\, and how artistic 
 research can shape the future tools\, interfaces and methods of spatial so
 und creation. NOT JUST ARTWORKS: they explicitly say they want new methods
 \, insights and creative infrastructures too. Outcomes may include install
 ations\, spatial audio compositions\, performances\, audiovisual works\, s
 oftware prototypes\, reusable methodologies or modular interfaces. Residen
 ts may collaborate with a 4DSOUND software developer to prototype new tool
 s\, and practitioners who already build their own software\, hardware or c
 reative technologies are encouraged to advance those as part of the reside
 ncy. WHO: architects are the named invitees\, with scenographers\, spatial
  designers\, installation artists\, sound artists\, composers and creative
  technologists also encouraged. Creative coding experience is welcome but 
 not required. Lead researcher is Poul Holleman\, co-founder and director o
 f 4DSOUND. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherla
 nds AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). 
 This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listeni
 ng is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound\, immersive experienc
 e\, performance and installation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND
 \, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supp
 orted by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listenin
 g (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLocation: Amsterdam\, Net
 herlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.\nAward: Total residency fee of
  EUR 7\,125\, calculated as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285.
  Six of those 25 days can be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with thei
 r team. Some additional financial support is available if needed\, includi
 ng material and travel costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agree
 d before the residency starts. No application fee.\nDuration: December 202
 6 to February 2027\, structured as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 Septe
 mber 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture
 -on-sound/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture-on-sound/en?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
